WEBVTT

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:06.711
Hey guys, Ryan from Argentina here and this show really means a lot to me.
大家好，我是来自阿根廷的瑞安，这个节目对我的意义非常重大。

00:00:06.711 --> 00:00:10.178
What you've made here is absolutely a masterpiece.
你们在这里创作的作品绝对是一部杰作。

00:00:10.178 --> 00:00:15.517
I watch it when I'm sad, when I'm angry, when I'm happy, when I'm frustrated.
当我难过时、生气时、开心时、沮丧时，我都会看它。

00:00:15.517 --> 00:00:18.221
I've watched it for a lot of years now.
我看了很多年了。

00:00:18.221 --> 00:00:18.721
I'm 25.
我今年25岁。

00:00:18.721 --> 00:00:30.994
Last year I was diagnosed with autism, so I learned that this show had also helped me make some social skills and understand social cues in a way that I didn't quite understand.
去年我被诊断出患有自闭症，所以我意识到这个节目也帮助我掌握了一些社交技巧，并以一种我以前不太懂的方式理解了社交暗示。

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:40.028
It scratched that itch for me and it really has helped me with a lot of relationships right now and in the past as well.
它满足了我的那种需求，而且无论是在现在还是过去，它确实在很多方面帮我处理好了各种人际关系。

00:00:40.028 --> 00:00:44.100
It really has in some way shaped who I am as a person.
它在某种程度上确实塑造了我作为一个人的性格。

00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:49.460
You've made something really really beautiful here once again and thank you for making this show.
你们在这里再次创作了一些非常非常美好的东西，谢谢你们制作了这个节目。

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:09.709
in New York City.
在纽约市。

00:01:09.709 --> 00:01:13.069
I can't believe it, Craig, can you believe it?
我简直不敢相信，克雷格，你敢相信吗？

00:01:13.069 --> 00:01:14.310
I can't believe it.
我简直不敢相信。

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.150
We're here.
我们到了。

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:25.829
Here we are at the end of season one of How I Met Your Mother episode 22, appropriately titled The End.
我们迎来了《老爸老妈浪漫史》第一季的尾声，第22集，片名非常贴切，叫做《终局》。

00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:31.430
Welcome to the end of season one, but certainly not the end of all the way.
欢迎来到第一季的尾声，但绝不是整段旅程的终点。

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:53.709
We got eight more seasons to go, but welcome to How We Made Your Mother, where I, Josh Radner, who played Ted Mosby on all nine seasons of how I met your mother, and going back through the show with all these years later, with our older eyes and our wiser minds, with Craig Thomas, the co-creator of the show, along with Carter Bayes.
我们还有八季要走，不过欢迎来到《我们是如何创作老爸老妈浪漫史的》，在这里，我——乔什·拉德纳，在《老爸老妈浪漫史》全部九季中饰演泰德·莫斯比，带着多年后的阅历、更成熟的目光和更睿智的心态，与该剧的共同创作者克雷格·托马斯（另一位是卡特·贝斯）一起，重新回顾这部剧。

00:01:53.709 --> 00:01:55.060
Hi, Craig.
嗨，克雷格。

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:55.980
Hello, sir.
你好，先生。

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.060
Great to see you, as always.
很高兴见到你，一如既往。

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.659
Thrilled, we've made it to the end of season one.
我们竟然走到了第一季的尾声，真是太激动了。

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:02.379
How's this been for you?
这对你来说感觉怎么样？

00:02:02.379 --> 00:02:07.260
20 years later, looking back on all this, It's been a blast for me, but let's go and get into it.
20年后再回首这一切，对我来说这是一段非常美妙的经历，不过我们还是直接切入正题吧。

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:13.180
Point of interest, the episode's actually called come on and not the end, but the end might be a better title.
顺便提一下，这一集的真实片名其实叫《来吧》，而不是《终局》，不过《终局》也许是个更好的名字。

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.340
And I think it's on theme because we've pitched better titles for many of the episodes that we've done.
而且我觉得这很符合我们的风格，因为我们为拍过的许多剧集都想过更好的片名。

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.900
We're like, yeah, come on, come on, come on.
我们当时就会说，对啊，来吧，来吧，来吧。

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.740
It's a pretty good title.
这真的是个挺不错的片名。

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:20.580
It's pretty good.
挺不错的。

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.419
Come on, it works.
《来吧》，这名字行得通。

00:02:21.419 --> 00:02:22.699
Milk was slightly controversial.
《牛奶》那一集稍微有点争议。

00:02:22.699 --> 00:02:25.460
There were a few who were like, maybe we could've had a better title there.
有几个人觉得，也许我们当时能起个更好的名字。

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.659
Milk is the only one.
《牛奶》是唯一一个。

00:02:26.659 --> 00:02:28.860
Maybe zip, zip, zip, and milk were the two.
也许《拉链，拉链，拉链》和《牛奶》是那两个。

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.819
Although I do think you're right, the wedding should have been called plus one.
不过我确实觉得你说得对，婚礼那一集本该叫《加一》。

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:33.659
Plus one.
加一。

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:41.659
What you learn 20 years later is call it the thing that you're going to call it 20 years later.
20年后你学到的一点就是：直接用你20年后打算叫的名字。

00:02:41.659 --> 00:02:44.919
Call it that in the moment and that will be easier 20 years later.
当下就这么叫，20年后就会轻松得多。

00:02:44.919 --> 00:02:49.060
Let your future self name the episodes.
让未来的自己来给剧集命名吧。

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.699
Let older wiser future you name your episodes.
让更成熟、更睿智的未来的你来为剧集命名吧。

00:02:51.699 --> 00:02:53.960
So Alec, tell us when this aired.
那么亚历克，告诉我们这一集是什么时候播出的。

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.759
When did a season episode 22 of season one air?
第一季第22集是什么时候播出的？

00:02:57.759 --> 00:03:02.400
It aired on May 15, 2006.
它于2006年5月15日播出。

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.099
And you, Craig, let us know.
还有你，克雷格，告诉我们一下。

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:08.860
Give us just a bare bones description of what happens in this episode.
用最简练的语言描述一下这一集发生了什么。

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.139
This was the description on Hulu.
这是Hulu上的剧情简介。

00:03:11.139 --> 00:03:15.159
I think I watched a plug for How Much Mother and how to watch it in America.
我想我看过一则关于《老爸老妈浪漫史》以及如何在美国观看它的广告。

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:16.620
You can watch it on Hulu or Netflix.
你可以在Hulu或Netflix上观看。

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.620
And I've been jumping back and forth between the two as we go through the season.
随着我们回顾这一季，我一直在两者之间来回切换。

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.259
So that's my little plug for How To Watch How Much Mother on Hulu.
所以这就是我为如何在Hulu上观看《老爸老妈浪漫史》做的一点小广告。

00:03:22.259 --> 00:03:25.219
This was the description of Come On.
这是《来吧》一集的简介。

00:03:25.219 --> 00:03:28.580
And it sounds like AI wrote something wrong.
听起来就像是人工智能写错了东西。

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:29.740
It sounds like something glitched.
听起来像是出了什么故障。

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:30.740
Here's the description.
剧情简介是这样的。

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.900
episode 22, come on, Ted uses a randance to keep Robin from camping.
第22集，《来吧》，泰德用雨舞阻止罗宾去露营。

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.699
It's just like, it's like, did something glitch out there?
这感觉就像，就好像，哪里出故障了吗？

00:03:41.699 --> 00:03:42.533
Is that it's height?
是它太高了吗？

00:03:42.533 --> 00:03:43.699
Are there types of involved?
里面有什么错别字吗？

00:03:43.699 --> 00:03:45.780
If you're like, oh, I've heard good things about this show.
如果你听到别人说，哦，我听说这部剧很不错。

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.431
What's this episode?
这是哪一集？

00:03:47.431 --> 00:03:48.340
Wait, what?
等等，什么？

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.620
Ted uses a randance to keep Robin from camping.
泰德用雨舞阻止罗宾去露营。

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:52.099
Yeah.
对。

00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:53.139
I guess that says it.
我想这就是它的意思。

00:03:53.139 --> 00:03:53.939
It's true.
这是真的。

00:03:53.939 --> 00:03:54.819
It's all true.
这都是真的。

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:58.659
All those words are true, but it's a weird little haiku.
所有这些词都是真的，但它读起来像个奇怪的小俳句。

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:00.099
It's been distilled down to there.
剧情就被提炼成了这样。

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:03.659
It raises more questions than it answers, as they say.
正如他们所说，它引发的问题比解答的问题还要多。

00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:04.659
Yeah.
对。

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:08.900
So, so Robin's going to go camping with Sandy Rivers.
所以，罗宾要和桑迪·里弗斯去露营。

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.300
Robin's going to go camping with Sandy Rivers.
罗宾要和桑迪·里弗斯去露营。

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.659
She's learning that he is moving on from being her co-anchor.
她得知他不再担任她的联合主播了。

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:15.780
He's going to, he got a big job at CNN.
他要去CNN谋得一份高薪工作。

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.660
And now he's saying, hey, let's finally get together.
现在他说，嘿，我们终于可以在一起了吧。

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.259
You have a no-dating co-workers thing.
你们有个“不准和同事约会”的规定。

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:25.300
So there's this threat that Robin can end up with this other guy who's kind of horrible and ridiculous.
所以这就产生了一个威胁，罗宾可能会跟这个既糟糕又荒唐的其他男人在一起。

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.740
There's a little broadcast news going on here, don't you think?
这里面有点像电影《收播新闻》的感觉，你不觉得吗？

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.060
There's definitely broadcast news.
这绝对有《收播新闻》的影子。

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:36.939
I found myself thinking about that great movie for you young kids out there, watch the movie, broadcast news from the 80s, hilarious and heartfelt comedy.
我不禁想起年轻时的各位朋友们都看过的、那部来自80年代的伟大电影《收播新闻》，那是一部既搞笑又走心的喜剧。

00:04:36.939 --> 00:04:44.220
And elements of this, there is the really like pretty boy newscaster guy played by William Hart very brilliantly.
而其中的某些元素，比如威廉·赫特非常出色地饰演的那个超级大帅哥新闻主播。

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.100
And does he have any substance at all?
他身上有任何内涵吗？

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.459
Is sort of the question of that movie.
这可以说是那部电影探讨的问题。

00:04:48.459 --> 00:04:51.899
And Sander Rivers is very similar to that.
而桑迪·里弗斯跟那个角色非常相似。

00:04:51.899 --> 00:04:57.500
So yeah, there's this thread is Robin going to go with this other guy or does Ted still have a chance with her.
所以，是的，这里有这样一条线索：罗宾是要跟这个别的男人走，还是说泰德对她仍然有机会。

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:20.100
and Marshall and Lily, Marshall discovers that Lily has without telling him applied to this month's long art internship in San Francisco that would interfere with their wedding, which sends their wedding and their whole relationship into an existential crisis right as Ted is also trying to kind of see if he can win Robin over before Sandy Rivers gets his chance.
还有马歇尔和莉莉，马歇尔发现莉莉在没有告诉他的情况下，申请了旧金山一个长达数月的艺术实习项目，这将会干扰他们的婚礼，从而把他们的婚礼和整段感情都推向了一场存在主义危机，而此时泰德也正试图看看自己能否在桑迪·里弗斯抢先之前赢得罗宾的芳心。

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:27.980
And his best way of doing that is to make it rain, to watch out, rain out the company camping trip that Metro News 1 is about to have.
而他做到这一点的最好方法就是人工降雨，注意了，就是用雨水浇灭都市新闻一台即将成行的公司露营之旅。

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:32.180
So yes, Hulu distilled it down to one beautiful, perfect sentence there.
所以，是的，Hulu把它浓缩成了一个优美、完美的句子。

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:42.699
So how's it been, how's older, why does their future Craig feeling about present day, 2005, six, Craig, who made the first season of the show?
那么感觉如何，更成熟、更睿智的未来的克雷格，对2005到2006年制作了该剧第一季的当年的克雷格作何感想？

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:45.740
I've loved rewatching season one.
我很喜欢重新观看第一季。

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:54.579
And by the way, I'm wearing my, if anyone's watching this, I'm wearing my big foot shirt in honor of martial arts in honor of our final episode of season one for the podcast.
顺便说一句，如果有人在看这个视频的话，为了向马歇尔致敬，为了向我们播客的第一季最后一集致敬，我穿着我的大脚怪衬衫。

00:05:54.579 --> 00:05:55.620
I love this, Josh.
我太喜欢了，乔什。

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.459
It's been amazing.
这感觉太奇妙了。

00:05:56.459 --> 00:06:00.699
I really had not rewatched the show since it ended.
自从剧集结束以来，我真的再也没有重温过它。

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:07.379
I had not, I had big, a couple episodes here and there, Thanksgiving, Thanksgiving, or someone, you know, someone wanted to watch this, watch that.
我没有，我只是偶尔看过一两集，比如感恩节那一集，或者有人想看这个、看那个的时候。

00:06:07.379 --> 00:06:12.459
I've not done this, I've not gone back through and a lot of these I hadn't seen in years and years and years.
我没有这样做过，我没有从头到尾重温，其中很多集我真的已经好多年没看过。

00:06:12.459 --> 00:06:18.699
And I'm loving it, some of it plays very different, three, 20 years older eyes and other parts of it.
我很喜欢这种感觉，带着年长20岁的目光去看，有些部分的感觉大不相同，而另一些部分。

00:06:18.699 --> 00:06:20.699
I'm just right back in that moment.
我又一下子回到了那个时刻。

00:06:20.699 --> 00:06:23.060
And I can remember it precisely what that moment felt like.
我能精确地记起当时那种感觉是怎样的。

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:23.899
What about you?
你呢？

00:06:23.899 --> 00:06:39.019
Well, you know, someone told me, I don't remember who it was, but if someone on the show said, during first season, we're gonna look back on these and it's gonna look pretty raw, pretty, like we are finding our footing, we are finding our vibe, our voice, all that.
嗯，你知道的，有人跟我说过——我不记得是谁了，但剧组里有人在第一季的时候说过，我们以后回过头来看这些会觉得挺粗糙的，挺像我们在摸索方向、寻找自己的基调、声音以及所有这一切的。

00:06:39.019 --> 00:06:50.500
And I think on some level that's true, you look back and you say, oh, we figured out how to do this better or this better, but I was startled at how fully formed the show was from the jump.
我觉得从某种程度上讲这是真的，你回过头看会说，哦，我们当时懂得了怎么把这个或者那个拍得更好，但我还是被这部剧从一开始就展现出的成熟度给震惊到了。

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:57.540
Like the show gets funnier, the stakes get higher, the characters deepen, we figured a lot of stuff out.
比如节目变得越来越有趣，赌注越来越高，角色更加立体，我们摸索出了很多门道。

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:06.699
You figured out how to mine more comedy, like you figured out more comedy for Robin, different comedic tracks for Ted that weren't there in the first season.
你们摸索出了如何挖掘更多喜剧效果，比如为罗宾创造了更多笑料，为泰德设计了第一季中没有的不同喜剧路线。

00:07:06.699 --> 00:07:11.699
But I was surprised at how many bangers are in first season.
但我惊讶于第一季里竟然有那么多精彩绝伦的亮点。

00:07:11.699 --> 00:07:14.779
Like there's some classic episodes in first season.
比如第一季里有一些非常经典的剧集。

00:07:14.779 --> 00:07:22.860
And I think that if you watch all 22 episodes, I understand some more of the like stickiness of the show.
而且我认为如果你看完所有22集，我能更理解这部剧那种让人欲罢不能的粘性。

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.779
Like there was that episode where Jordana turned to me and she was like, I'm in.
就像乔丹娜转过身对我说的那集一样，她说：“我入坑了。”

00:07:25.779 --> 00:07:29.699
Like I'm emotionally invested in these characters.
就像我对这些角色产生了情感投入。

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:32.060
I wanna figure this out.
我想弄清楚这一切。

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:34.180
I wanna keep journeying with these people.
我想继续和这些人一起经历这段旅程。

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:41.620
And I think for a first episode of the first season of a television show, it did exactly literally what a TV show is supposed to do.
我认为对于一部电视剧的第一季的第一集来说，它完美地做到了电视剧应该做的事情。

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.620
It weaseled its way under your heart.
它悄悄钻进了你的心坎里。

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:45.259
It tickles your funny bone.
它触动了你的笑神经。

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:53.699
it makes you wanna spend at least 22 minutes a week with these people who are now a lot more than that if you can watch it at your own pace.
它让你每周至少愿意花22分钟和这些人在一起，如果你按自己的节奏观看，时间甚至会远远不止这些。

00:07:53.699 --> 00:08:01.810
But I think it was like, I'm startled at how successful it was just in the basics of what a first season of television is supposed to do.
但我当时想的是，它在完成电视剧第一季本该做到的基础工作时，能取得如此成功，这让我大吃一惊。

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:04.970
Yeah, I'm so glad it still feels that way.
是的，我很高兴它现在感觉依然如此。

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:10.370
I, similarly, I'm impressed by what we could cram into 22 minutes.
同样地，我对我们在22分钟里能塞进那么多内容感到印象深刻。

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:16.490
Is that there's some of these episodes, including the one we're gonna talk about today, come on the season family where I'm like, I can't believe how much is in there.
其中有些剧集，包括我们今天要谈论的这一集，也就是我们这季要聊的这一集，让我觉得：“天哪，我简直不敢相信里面有这么多内容。”

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:21.689
In 22 minutes, I mean, every single episode, I wish was 34 minutes or 45 minutes.
在22分钟里，我是说，我希望每一集都有34分钟或45分钟。

00:08:21.689 --> 00:08:28.769
Every episode, I'd say that's the hardest thing to watch is just to rewatch it and go, oh, so many of those I would have loved who have been a little longer.
每一集都是如此，我常说最难受的就是重温的时候心想：“哦，这里面有好多内容，如果能再长一点就好了。”

00:08:28.769 --> 00:08:35.529
But given that parameter, 22 minutes on the nose, basically, that's what a half hour has become.
但考虑到这个限制，基本上就是正正好22分钟，这也是现在半小时节目的标准长度。

00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:39.850
My wife and I are rewatching MASH from the 1970s and they're like 25, 26 minutes long.
我和我妻子正在重温20世纪70年代的《陆军流动医院》，它们大概有25、26分钟长。

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:48.049
And every time I see that time code come up that says that, I'm like, I would have killed for those extras for your four minutes, the huge three or four minutes.
每次我看到时间码显示那个长度时，我都想，为了那多出来的四分钟、那宝贵的整整三四分钟，我愿意付出一切。

00:08:48.049 --> 00:09:01.169
But that said, there is also, there's creativity, right, and necessities, the mother of invention, and you make the container the size that it is, and you say it has to fit in this container, there is invention and creativity that comes out of that.
话虽如此，这里面也有创造力，对吧，穷则思变，你把容器做成固定的大小，然后说它必须塞进这个容器里，由此会产生发明创造和无限创意。

00:09:01.169 --> 00:09:06.970
I'm very impressed by the daring of how much we crammed into that 22-minute container, all of us.
我非常赞赏我们所有人那种敢想敢干的魄力，竟然把那么多内容硬塞进了那个22分钟的容器里。

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:22.730
You can see as the series goes on, you know how we've kind of been tracking, You know, I think the first time we really noticed you guys leaning into, oh, it's a narrator and he's telling the story and he can forget a detail, you know, Marshall's crown in okay awesome.
随着剧集的推进，你可以看到，正如我们之前所探讨的那样，你知道的，我想我们第一次真正注意到你们开始倾向于这种设定——哦，这里有个旁白，他在讲故事，而且他可能会漏掉一个细节，你知道的，比如马歇尔在《完全棒极了》里面的皇冠。

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:23.730
Like, oh, oh, I forgot.
就像是：“哦，哦，我忘了。”

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:24.730
There was a there's a detail.
“这里有一个细节。”

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:25.730
I forgot to tell you.
“我忘记告诉你们了。”

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:26.730
Right?
对吧？

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:39.850
Or, you know, there's just these different things where you guys really leaned into the narrator because think about it, if you're telling your friends a story, but one of them has to go in 12 minutes, you'll tell the story in 12 minutes.
或者，你知道的，就是有这些不同的地方，你们真正开始依赖旁白这个设定，因为仔细想想，如果你在给朋友讲一个故事，但其中一个人12分钟后就得离开，你就会在12分钟内把故事讲完。

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:44.169
like, you'll figure out a way to compress the story to get it told.
你会想办法压缩故事把它讲完。

00:09:44.169 --> 00:09:47.610
And I think that, yeah, 25 minutes would have been great.
我想，是的，25分钟当然会很棒。

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:53.250
There would have been some, but also you might have looked back and said, wow, we could have taken three minutes out of that episode.
可能会有一些额外的内容，但反过来你可能也会回过头来说：“哇，我们其实可以从那一集里剪掉三分钟。”

00:09:53.250 --> 00:10:01.169
Like, I think there's something, I think I mentioned this, but Leonard Bernstein said, you know, what are the necessary ingredients to create a work of genius?
就像我觉得，我好像提到过这个，伦纳德·伯恩斯坦曾说过，创造一部天才之作的必要元素是什么？

00:10:01.169 --> 00:10:03.490
You need a great idea and not quite enough time.
你需要一个伟大的创意，外加一点点不够用的时间。

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:05.970
And we didn't have quite enough time, right?
而我们当时确实没有足够的时间，对吧？

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:18.289
like it forced us into some very interesting narrative, daring narrative choices that, you know, you look back and again, it's an imperfect, perfect process.
这迫使我们做出了一些非常有趣的叙事选择、很大胆的叙事选择，你回过头来看，这又是一个不完美的完美过程。

00:10:18.289 --> 00:10:21.649
It was an imperfect, perfect show or a perfectly imperfect show.
它是一部不完美的完美剧集，或者说是一部完美的不完美剧集。

00:10:21.649 --> 00:10:38.049
And I think like you look back, I'm looking back not just at my performance but the whole show as a kind of warts and all lovable thing, you know, that you much like a, you know, like a child or someone you love, you love not because you love in spite of.
我想当你回看时，我不仅是在回顾我的表演，而是在回顾整部剧，带着一种“瑕不掩瑜”的可爱感，你知道的，就像对一个孩子或你爱的人那样，你爱他们不是因为完美，而是不计缺点地去爱。

00:10:38.049 --> 00:10:39.610
You love all of it.
你爱他们的全部。

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:49.929
And I wrote you this little ditty to send to you in New York City.
我为你写了这首小曲，要发到纽约市去给你。

00:10:49.929 --> 00:10:53.700
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:54.899
Hey, I'm Carl Radke.
嘿，我是卡尔·拉德克。

00:10:54.899 --> 00:11:03.500
You might know me from Bravo's Summer House, but on my podcast, More Life, I get your favorite reality stars off the clock and into the conversations you don't usually get to hear.
你可能从 Bravo 的《夏日山庄》里认识我，但在我的播客《更多生活》（More Life）中，我会让你们喜爱的真人秀明星们下班放松，开启那些你平时听不到的对话。

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:06.940
the deep stuff, the vulnerable stuff, the uncomfortable stuff.
那些深刻的内容、脆弱的心事、让人局促不安的话题。

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:12.779
I've sat down with Sierra Miller, Derinda Medley, Tom Schwartz, Lindsey Hubbard, and a whole lot more.
我曾与席亚拉·米勒、多琳达·梅德利、汤姆·施瓦茨、林赛·哈勃等等许多人坐在一起畅谈。

00:11:12.779 --> 00:11:18.299
We get into relationships, growing up, the messy stuff, and everything that happens when the cameras aren't rolling.
我们探讨人际关系、成长经历、那些剪不断理还乱的麻烦事，以及摄像机关机后发生的一切。

00:11:18.299 --> 00:11:23.580
New episodes of More Life every week, listen wherever you get your podcasts, or watch on YouTube.
每周都有《更多生活》的新节目上线，无论你在哪里听播客都可以收听，或者在YouTube上观看。

00:11:30.309 --> 00:11:32.409
And now, back to the show.
现在，让我们回到节目中。

00:11:32.409 --> 00:11:40.860
I think I've been avoiding rewatching it sometimes, thinking I'll be harsh on it, I'll judge it, see where there could have been better jokes.
我想我有时一直在逃避重温这部剧，以为自己会对它很苛刻，会去评判它，看看哪些地方本可以有更好的笑话。

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:48.954
In fact, joke, we never beat in that scene, that blow to that scene, the ending of a scene that I always thought could have been better.
事实上，有些笑料，我们在那一场戏里从未真正攻克，那个让整场戏戛然而止的包袱，也就是一场戏的结尾，我一直觉得本可以处理得更好。

00:11:48.954 --> 00:11:53.179
It's going to bother me from 15 years ago, and it just fucking doesn't.
这件事本该让我这个15年前的人耿耿于怀，但它他妈的根本没有。

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:57.523
It's so interesting that you go back and there's so much more generosity.
很有意思的是，当你回过头来看时，你会带着多得多的宽容。

00:11:57.523 --> 00:12:11.330
I have such a better ability to see the energy of the thing, the beating heart of the thing, the essential important core of what it is, and any little details, it could have been a little bit this way or a little bit more that way.
我有了更强能力去看到整部作品的能量、它的跳动的心脏、它最本质最重要的核心，至于任何微小的细节——本可以稍微这样处理，或者稍微那样调整。

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:23.529
They just kind of come out in the wash and the thing is the thing and I'm really proud of how the soul of it is there and I think that has transcended any go-backy nitpickiness that I thought I would have.
这些都在随后的重温中烟消云散了，作品本身就是它原本的样子，我真的为它灵魂的内核得以保留而感到骄傲，我认为这已经超越了我原本以为自己会有的那种挑刺心态。

00:12:23.529 --> 00:12:29.399
It's so much about the energy and I think your energy in that performance of TED in season one is such a live liar.
这很大程度上关乎能量，我认为你在第一季中对泰德的演绎所展现出的能量，简直像一根点燃的引信。

00:12:29.399 --> 00:12:38.077
There's such a drive to TED and especially in this episode, come on where Ted literally makes it rain, literally has a supernatural power to make it rain in this episode.
泰德身上有一种强烈的驱动力，尤其是在这一集里，在《来吧》这一集里，泰德字面意义上地呼风唤雨，字面意义上拥有了在这一集里让雨停下来的超自然力量。

00:12:38.077 --> 00:12:43.539
There's something that I thought that really celebrated what Ted's energy has been through the whole thing.
我觉得这里面有一些内容，真正赞颂了泰德贯穿始终的那种能量。

00:12:43.539 --> 00:12:46.346
And everyone, it's just their energy is coming through.
而且每个人，他们的能量都全然展现了出来。

00:12:46.346 --> 00:12:47.622
It was such a great cast.
这是一支出色的演员阵容。

00:12:47.622 --> 00:12:48.796
You guys were so great.
你们太棒了。

00:12:48.796 --> 00:12:50.021
You were great together.
你们在一起的化学反应太棒了。

00:12:50.021 --> 00:12:56.044
You were great when you were featured when it was a little more of a Ted episode or a little more of a Lillie episode.
无论是由你主导、偏向泰德视角的剧集，还是偏向莉莉视角的剧集，你们的表演都非常精彩。

00:12:56.044 --> 00:13:03.905
We were talking about the last couple episodes of this season where Lillie kind of spiraling out and has this totally new kind of slaving unhinged energy.
我们一直在谈论这一季的最后几集，莉莉情绪有点失控，展现出了一种完全崭新的、近乎疯狂和崩溃的能量。

00:13:03.905 --> 00:13:13.093
Just getting to see what everybody can do episode to episode and you see us all as writers and as actors discovering what we can do and what it can be and figuring out these moves.
看着大家在一集又一集中展现各自的本领，看着我们所有身为主创和演员的人去探索我们能做到什么、作品能呈现出什么样貌，并摸索出这些表演的门道。

00:13:13.093 --> 00:13:17.482
And there's a real joy and curiosity and spark to that because you're watching people.
这其中有一种真正的喜悦、好奇心和火花，因为你在看着人们不断成长。

00:13:17.482 --> 00:13:22.076
It's like the part of the superhero movie where the superhero is discovering their powers.
这就像超级英雄电影里超级英雄正在发掘自己超能力的那一部分。

00:13:22.076 --> 00:13:22.995
Oh, I can do this.
“哦，我能做到这个。”

00:13:22.995 --> 00:13:23.506
I can fly.
“我会飞。”

00:13:23.506 --> 00:13:25.802
I can shoot lasers out of my eyes, it's that.
“我的眼睛能发射激光，”就是那种感觉。

00:13:25.802 --> 00:13:27.334
It's like, it's finding those.
就像是，在寻找这些能力。

00:13:27.334 --> 00:13:30.549
You're watching the show find it, find itself, find it's power.
你在看着这部剧寻找它自己，寻找它自己的定位，寻找它自己的力量。

00:13:30.549 --> 00:13:41.229
And then as the show goes, all of those tricks, like the expanded toolbox that you discovered in season one, you bring all that into season two.
然后随着剧集的推进，所有这些技巧，比如你在第一季中发掘出的不断扩展的工具箱，你把所有这些都带入了第二季。

00:13:41.229 --> 00:13:44.269
And you know, oh, we can go to telepathy.
你知道，“哦，我们可以用读心术。”

00:13:44.269 --> 00:13:46.154
That's a well we can go to.
那是我们可以信手拈来的招式。

00:13:46.154 --> 00:13:47.690
That's a kind of move.
那是某种表演手段。

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:59.781
We can compress time by having the narrator do something that will actually be really funny and sharp and say something about memory, say something about storytelling, say something about a father trying to print.
我们可以通过让旁白做一些既非常搞笑犀利、又能反映记忆本质、讲述故事的真谛、甚至展现一个父亲试图向……吐露心声的事情来压缩时间。

00:13:59.781 --> 00:14:13.350
You know, I don't think he did it as much in season one, but the knowledge that he's telling what like a 16 year old and a 14 year old, these stories, I don't know how old they were supposed to be, but that kind of like, we had sandwiches.
你知道，我觉得他在第一季里没怎么这样做，但他明知自己是在向一个16岁和一个14岁的孩子讲这些故事——我不知道他们当时设定应该是多大，但那种感觉就像是，“我们当时在吃三明治。”

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:20.780
That's what we were doing was eating sandwiches, you know, protecting their young ears from these things.
我们当时在做的事就是吃三明治，你知道的，这是为了保护他们年幼的耳朵免受那些事情的污染。

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:27.573
Yeah, it just occurred to me and I'm jumping way ahead, but I just want to remember to say this.
是的，我刚刚突然想到，虽然我有点跳跃得太远了，但我只是想记住把这个说出来。

00:14:27.573 --> 00:14:42.999
The phrase, come on, was something that had been, you know, littered throughout the show, but Ted said it a lot in a kind of like, come on, you know, like, come on, a very casual like, join me in my, join me over here.
“来吧”（Come on）这个短语，在剧中可以说是随处可见，但泰德经常用它来表达一种类似“得了吧，你知道的”，比如“来吧，加入我的行列，到我这边来。”

00:14:42.999 --> 00:14:44.768
Just just step over here.
“就往这边走一步。”

00:14:44.768 --> 00:14:51.349
Come But, in this episode, he is shouting to the cosmos, come on, give me what I need, right?
“来吧……”但是，在这集里，他是对着苍穹在大喊：“来吧，把我要的东西给我，好吗？”

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:55.230
It's so much less of a seduction and more of a demand.
这与其说是一种诱导，不如说是一种强烈索取。

00:14:55.230 --> 00:15:06.549
I remember I had a meditation teacher who said, if you read great prayerful people, they never are delicate with the Lord.
我记得我曾有一位冥想老师说过，如果你阅读那些伟大的祈祷者的文字，他们在面对上天时从不小心翼翼。

00:15:06.549 --> 00:15:10.429
They never say, oh, it's not too much trouble.
他们从不说：“哦，这应该不算太麻烦吧？”

00:15:10.429 --> 00:15:16.669
Could you please slay my enemies and could you please deliver my people?
“您能不能大发慈悲消灭我的敌人，顺便拯救我的子民？”

00:15:16.669 --> 00:15:18.830
No, if you're busy, I understand.
“不，如果您很忙的话，我完全理解。”

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:29.669
If you read the Psalms of David, if you read the prophets, there's an almost like ordering slay my enemies, you know, deliver my people.
如果你读《大卫诗篇》，如果你读先知们的文字，那里面几乎是一种命令式的语气——“消灭我的敌人”，你知道的，“拯救我的子民”。

00:15:29.669 --> 00:15:33.669
Like you'd be very clear with divinity, what you need.
就像你要向神明非常明确地表达你需要什么一样。

00:15:33.669 --> 00:15:41.237
And I think the reason the rain dance works is because Ted, it's almost like the cosmos recognize like a pure request.
我认为祈雨舞之所以管用，是因为泰德的诉求，几乎就像是苍穹识别出这是一种纯粹的请求。

00:15:41.237 --> 00:15:44.251
They're like, we're gonna give this one to him.
它们会觉得：“我们把这个满足他吧。”

00:15:44.251 --> 00:15:45.470
Like he needs this.
“他需要这个。”

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:46.536
He wants this.
“他渴望这个。”

00:15:46.536 --> 00:15:48.136
He's earned this one.
“他配得上这个。”

00:15:48.136 --> 00:15:48.636
Yeah.
是的。

00:15:48.636 --> 00:15:59.989
And I loved, and I know this is jumping to the end, but I loved, I had forgotten the euphoria and the joy at that right, what that rain means to Ted.
我特别喜欢——我知道这话题跳到了结尾，但我真的很喜欢，我差点忘了那种狂喜，以及那种雨对泰德来说意味着什么的快乐。

00:16:00.669 --> 00:16:08.762
And how just every raindrop that he gets pelted with is just the universe is on my side, the universe wants this to happen.
每一个打在他身上的雨滴都在诉说：宇宙正站在我这边，宇宙希望这件事发生。

00:16:08.762 --> 00:16:10.473
I am receiving a blessing.
我正在接受神的赐福。

00:16:10.473 --> 00:16:16.131
And there is something not to get to theological about it, but it's how my mind works.
这里面有些东西，虽然不想把它弄得太神学化，但这就是我脑子运转的方式。

00:16:16.131 --> 00:16:19.881
There is something baptismal and renewing about the rain.
雨有一种洗礼和让人焕然一新的感觉。

00:16:19.881 --> 00:16:28.830
There's something holy about the rain or the flood or the washing away of the old and the the the birthing and the welcoming of the new.
雨水、洪水、洗去陈旧、孕育并迎接新生，这些都带有一种神圣感。

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:34.245
That final moment with an errandary said everything had changed and rain is changed.
那个讲述一切都已改变的结局时刻，雨也随之改变了。

00:16:34.245 --> 00:16:35.163
Water is changed.
水改变了。

00:16:35.163 --> 00:16:37.971
It's a baptism, but it also washes some things away.
这是一场洗礼，但它也冲走了一些东西。

00:16:37.971 --> 00:16:41.588
And Marshall and Millie are a little bit washed away by this flood.
马歇尔和莉莉也被这场洪水冲走了一部分。

00:16:41.588 --> 00:16:51.577
In this moment, as Ted is sort of reborn into this whole new chapter and the idea of a chapter ending in a way that is sad for Marshall and a chapter beginning that is euphoric for Ted.
在这个时刻，泰德仿佛重生到了一个全新的篇章，而对马歇尔来说，一个篇章的结束带着悲伤，对泰德来说，一个篇章的开始则是狂喜的。

00:16:51.577 --> 00:16:54.817
Oh my God, your performance and that was so wonderful, Josh.
天哪，乔治，你在那场戏里的表演太棒了。

00:16:54.817 --> 00:16:59.028
Those two paths kind of crossing as one kind of trends up and one trends down.
这两条轨迹交织在一起，一条呈上升趋势，另一条呈下降趋势。

00:16:59.028 --> 00:17:03.510
That is one of my favorite moments of of season one and maybe of the entire series.
那是第一季中最让我喜欢的时刻之一，也许也是整部剧中最喜欢的时刻之一。

00:17:03.509 --> 00:17:04.349
Alec?
亚历克？

00:17:04.349 --> 00:17:17.069
Well, I'll just use the word earn, and that was exactly what I was gonna ask you about that I felt like you earned this bit of magic, this bit of miracle through the 21 episodes that came before it.
嗯，我只想用“赢得”这个词，这也是我正想问你的，我觉得通过前面21集的铺垫，你赢得了这丝魔法，赢得了这个奇迹。

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:24.990
You already broke some rules as Ted did by saying I love you on the first date and the first episode.
你已经打破了一些规则，就像泰德在第一集的第一次约会时就说我爱你那样。

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:32.549
But then you got all the way to and now I can make it rain, which of course would not have worked in the first episode.
但接着你一路走来，现在你能呼风唤雨了，这在第一集里当然是行不通的。

00:17:32.549 --> 00:17:36.309
That would have been what show am I watching, that this can happen.
那样的话大家就会想我到底在看什么剧，这种事居然能发生。

00:17:36.309 --> 00:17:50.670
But whether it's the telepathy or the jumping out of the window that doesn't there or the I don't remember, all these things built up to this moment where, okay, now you have all of this, you've built this up, you've earned this, what are you gonna do with it?
但不管是心灵感应还是跳窗户，不管有没有发生，或者我不记得了，所有这些事情都铺垫到了这一刻，好吧，现在你拥有了这一切，你做好了铺垫，你赢得了这一切，你打算怎么用它？

00:17:50.670 --> 00:18:03.609
And you just did something huge, and something huge for the character, for the characters and for the story going forward in this moment that you've reached the finale of season one, and now you can do something like this.
你刚刚做了一件大事，对于这个角色、这些人物以及故事的发展来说都是一件大事，此时你迎来了第一季的尾声，现在你可以做出这样的举动了。

00:18:03.609 --> 00:18:11.250
Yeah, yeah, I wrote down in my little notes for this episode, I wrote, Ted is entertainingly crazy here.
对，对，我在为这一集写的简短笔记里写道，泰德在这里疯狂得很有趣。

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:12.410
He's one of the notes I wrote down.
这是我写下的笔记之一。

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:20.369
Because he's like, he really is, there is this kind of euphoric, like it is its magic, right?
因为他确实如此，有一种狂喜的感觉，就像它是魔法一样，对吧？

00:18:20.369 --> 00:18:24.410
He's conjuring up magic on a rooftop in the middle of a storm.
他在暴风雨中的屋顶上施展魔法。

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:27.730
I mean, it really, and the energy Josh you had in that whole sequence.
我是说，真的，还有乔治你在整个序列中展现出的能量。

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:33.250
And when it started to rain and the joy and the freedom, like how did that feel acting that whole sequence?
当雨开始下的时候，那种喜悦和释放，演完整段戏是什么感觉？

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:38.569
Because I sensed you, I think you're doing amazing work through that whole last couple minutes of that.
因为我能感觉到你，我觉得在最后那几分钟里，你的表现真的太棒了。

00:18:38.569 --> 00:18:43.369
There's something really shifted in you and energetically there that was wonderful.
你身上真的有什么东西转变了，那种能量非常棒。

00:18:43.369 --> 00:18:53.410
Well, I can answer that, but I do wanna say, I find it amazing that Barney is the one on the roof with him saying, you're an idiot, what are you doing?
嗯，我可以回答这个问题，但我必须说，我觉得很有意思的是，巴尼竟然是那个陪他在屋顶上对他说“你是个神经病，你在干什么”的人。

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:55.170
But Barney's a magician.
但巴尼是个魔术师。

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:59.130
like Barney's really into magic, but he's into sleight of hand.
比如巴尼真的很喜欢魔术，但他喜欢的是手法魔术。

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:04.130
He's into being the god of his trip, right?
他喜欢成为自己世界的主宰，对吧？

00:19:04.329 --> 00:19:09.490
Like he's the one kind of tricking people and stunning people.
就像他是那种捉弄别人、惊艳别人的人。

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:14.519
Ted is actually invoking a kind of like cosmic magic trick.
而泰德实际上是在召唤一种宇宙级的魔术。

00:19:16.279 --> 00:19:19.960
He's petitioning something much bigger than himself.
他在向比自己宏大得多的存在祈求。

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:26.573
But the things, if you look back, and again, someone could write a college paper, paper on this, or ask C.P.T.
但如果你回过头来看——再说一次，有人可能会为此写一篇大学论文，或者让ChatGPT帮他们写一篇大学论文，但你知道的，孩子们别这么做。

00:19:26.573 --> 00:19:31.202
to write a college paper on this for them, but you know, don't do that, kids.
别那么做。

00:19:31.202 --> 00:19:32.044
Don't do that.
别那么做。

00:19:32.044 --> 00:19:49.540
But what I'm saying, I think that one of the things about how we make it, whether it's a recognizable university, it honors Newtonian laws, but every once in a while, it dips into this quantum magical cosmic zone where magic exists, where literal magic exists, a rain dance, he made it rain.
但我想说的是，关于我们如何创造它，无论是符合牛顿定律的、为人所熟知的大学物理，还是偶尔涉足魔法存在的量子魔术宇宙，字面意义上的魔法，求雨舞，他真的求来了雨。

00:19:49.540 --> 00:20:00.182
And I think there's something sweet about him telling his kids, you know, there's that Einstein quote, like you just have to decide if this is a friendly universe or not, right?
我觉得有一点很温馨，那就是他对他的孩子们说，你知道，爱因斯坦有那样一句名言：你只需要决定这究竟是一个友善的宇宙还是不友善的宇宙，对吧？

00:20:00.182 --> 00:20:12.867
And Jordana has talked about like that's the role, especially of the mother in the early years, that her face should reflect, this is a friendly universe, like you're wanted here, you're loved, this is friendly.
约尔达娜曾谈到过，这正是母亲在孩子早年扮演的角色，她的面部表情应该反映出：这是一个友善的宇宙，这里欢迎你，你被爱着，这一切都是友善的。

00:20:12.867 --> 00:20:18.759
But I think in some ways, Ted is also trying to give his kids the gift of it's a magical universe.
但我认为在某种程度上，泰德也是在给他的孩子们一份礼物，让他们相信这是一个充满魔法的宇宙。

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:21.207
There's magic in the universe.
宇宙中存在着魔法。

00:20:21.207 --> 00:20:25.698
If your belief is strong enough, if your intention is pure, if you're aligned.
如果你的信念足够坚定，如果你的意图纯粹，如果你与之同频。

00:20:25.698 --> 00:20:27.079
There's magic, you know?
你知道的，魔法是存在的。

00:20:27.079 --> 00:20:29.438
What did it feel like to act to that?
演这场戏是什么感觉？

00:20:29.438 --> 00:20:38.680
Just that ending sequence where like it works, it rains and the euphoria and the abandon, the kind of joyful abandon, and they're very confident.
仅仅是那个结局序列，它成功了，下雨了，那种狂喜与纵情，那种欢乐的纵情，他们非常自信。

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:41.749
It's very crazy, but it's very confidently crazy.
这非常疯狂，但却是一种充满自信的疯狂。

00:20:41.749 --> 00:20:43.880
It's a great energy you hit there.
你当时迸发出的能量太棒了。

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:45.603
What did that feel like?
那是什么感觉？

00:20:45.603 --> 00:20:59.893
I remember, I think Carter, like with smiling on set one day and he had just come from either maybe breaking the story, or he knew I was about to read it, and he said, Ted's going to do a rain dance.
我记得，好像卡特有一天在片场笑得很开心，他刚刚把这个剧情构思出来，或者他知道我快要读到这一集了，他对我说：“泰德要跳求雨舞了。”

00:20:59.893 --> 00:21:01.832
He's going to make it rain.
“他要让天上下雨。”

00:21:01.832 --> 00:21:04.345
And I just remember going, awesome.
我只记得当时反应是：太棒了。

00:21:04.345 --> 00:21:04.992
How cool.
太酷了。

00:21:04.992 --> 00:21:07.290
This is exactly the perfect Ted.
这正是最完美的泰德。

00:21:07.289 --> 00:21:09.104
Yeah, this is perfect.
是的，这太完美了。

00:21:09.104 --> 00:21:16.032
And he was so excited at that twist, and I was very interested in magical Ted Mosby.
他对这个反转感到无比兴奋，我也对富有魔法色彩的泰德·莫斯比非常感兴趣。

00:21:16.032 --> 00:21:29.970
That was my favorite Ted, was like the one who could control the elements and it wasn't that he controlled them because he actually knew how to do invocations and magic.
那是我最喜欢的泰德，那个能够控制自然元素的人，这倒不是因为他真的懂什么召唤术和魔法。

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:43.539
He did it because it was sheer force of will, you know, and I think that there's that great quote that says, you know, once you truly decide, Providence rises up to support you.
他做到这一点是因为纯粹的意志力，你知道的，我觉得有一句名言说得很好：一旦你真正下了决心，上天就会起来支持你。

00:21:43.539 --> 00:21:49.440
Like once the decision is fully made, divinity or Providence is on your side.
就像一旦决定彻底做出，神明或上天就会站在你这边。

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:55.960
And I feel like, I mean, how crazy to be on CBS in prime time and get to kind of act that out.
我觉得，在CBS黄金时段能把这种情节演出来，是多么疯狂的一件事啊。

00:21:55.960 --> 00:22:09.740
I just knew that it was cool and I knew that it was cool in the same way that I was very interested in esoteric spirituality, not as interested as I got into it.
我只知道这很酷，而且我知道这很酷，就像我对秘传灵性很感兴趣一样，虽然没有后来那么着迷。

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:18.440
But I was a meditator, I was interested in what's beyond the unseen, you know, what's behind the veil.
但我当时在练习冥想，我对看不见的世界背后有什么很感兴趣，你知道的，面纱后面是什么。

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:20.079
I've always been curious about that.
我对这些一直都很好奇。

00:22:20.079 --> 00:22:23.240
And I thought that was something when people say, how are you like Ted?
当人们问我“你哪里像泰德”时，我会觉得，嗯，我相信一个充满魔法的宇宙。

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:25.559
I'll be like, well, I believe in a magical universe.
我是说我确实相信，你知道的，我很享受剧中那些变得充满魔法和诡异的时刻。

00:22:25.559 --> 00:22:32.480
Like I do, you know, and I enjoy the moments when the show got magical and weird.
我觉得那是《老爸老妈浪漫史》中最让我喜爱的几个瞬间。

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:36.359
I thought it was like there's some of my favorite how I met your mother moments.
当然，泰德也经常被重力拉回地面。

00:22:36.359 --> 00:22:41.359
And certainly Ted was pulled down by gravity a lot.
当他落回地面时，他也是这么想的。

00:22:42.319 --> 00:22:44.660
And he thought it when he came back down.
但每次他总是拍拍灰尘。

00:22:45.839 --> 00:22:48.279
But he always dusted himself off.
重新跳起舞来。

00:22:48.279 --> 00:22:49.799
and danced again.
重新跳起舞来。

00:22:49.799 --> 00:22:54.519
I remember working with the choreographer who I think was Barney's cousin.
我记得曾和那位编舞合作过，我想她好像是巴尼的表妹。

00:22:54.519 --> 00:22:55.359
Oh yeah, right.
哦对，没错。

00:22:55.359 --> 00:22:56.200
It was her again.
又是她。

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:57.640
It was from, okay, I also, right?
是来自，好吧，我也，对吧？

00:22:57.640 --> 00:22:59.440
I forgot that she came back around.
我忘了她又回来了。

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:00.599
That's okay, yeah.
没关系，是的。

00:23:00.599 --> 00:23:08.079
I do remember we landed on moves that kind of like felt good in my body.
我确实记得我们最终确定了一些动作，让我身体感觉很舒服。

00:23:08.079 --> 00:23:19.119
And moves that were both felt like could be plausible as moves he would land on, but we're also funny and joyful to do.
这些动作既符合他会做出的合理举动，同时做起来又滑稽又快乐。

00:23:19.119 --> 00:23:28.680
I remember fingertips and that kind of like, almost like a running man, like I was hopping from foot to foot and going in circles.
我记得手指的动作，还有那种，几乎就像是在跳奔跑人舞（Running Man），我双脚交替跳跃，转着圈。

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:36.559
And it was like, it was ludicrous on some level, but the fact that it works makes it great, you know?
从某种程度上讲这很荒谬，但它确实管用，这就让它变得很棒，你知道吗？

00:23:36.559 --> 00:23:46.019
Like, and I don't know if it's the dance that works or his howl towards the heavens, is demand that it rained.
而且我不知道起作用的究竟是那段舞蹈，还是他对着天空的怒吼，是他对下雨的强烈要求。

00:23:46.019 --> 00:23:54.420
But I just feel like in that moment, like heaven smiles on him, the disguise open up for him, you know?
但我只觉得在那个时刻，上天向他微笑，天空为他敞开，你知道的？

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:57.900
He, what was the thing?
他，那是怎么说的来着？

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:09.099
Someone, I heard once someone said about astrology at a certain level of consciousness that stars control you, but at a higher level of consciousness, you control the stars.
我曾经听人说过，关于某种意识层面的占星术：在一定的意识水平下，星星控制着你；但在更高的意识水平下，你控制着星星。

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:12.369
Yeah, it's a great line.
是的，这是一句很棒的台词。

00:24:12.369 --> 00:24:22.490
And I think in that moment for whatever reason, he just, he seizes control of the elements through his enormous heart and his enormous will.
我想在那一刻，不管出于什么原因，他凭借自己博大的胸怀和坚定的意志，夺取了对自然元素的控制权。

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:33.930
And it doesn't always work because he's often foiled pretty spectacularly by the elements and by, you know, reality, just not agreeing with him.
事情并不总是如他所愿，因为他经常被自然元素和残酷的现实相当戏剧性地泼冷水，现实就是不买他的账。

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:35.890
But in this moment, it works.
但在这一个时刻，它成功了。

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:39.049
And I always found it to be completely charming.
我一直觉得这极其迷人。

00:24:39.049 --> 00:24:49.150
It's like, everybody has something in their life story or many things that in retrospect seem magical or seem impossible, seem improbable.
就像每个人的人生故事中都有那么一些东西，或者有很多东西，回想起来显得充满魔幻，或者显得不可能、不可思议。

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:52.210
And I think the magic is made in the storytelling, right?
我想魔法是在讲故事的过程中创造出来的，对吧？

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:53.890
Maybe it just rained.
也许只是下雨了。

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:57.009
Maybe it just happened to rain at that moment.
也许当时恰好就下雨了。

00:24:57.009 --> 00:25:00.970
But in Future Ted's story, he made it rain.
但在未来泰德的故事里，是他求来了雨。

00:25:00.970 --> 00:25:01.809
And who's right?
谁才是对的？

00:25:01.809 --> 00:25:04.450
Did it just rain or did Ted make it rain?
究竟只是下雨了，还是泰德求来了雨？

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:04.950
Yeah.
对。

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:06.202
The answer is yes.
答案是肯定的。

00:25:06.049 --> 00:25:07.289
The answer is yes.
答案是肯定的。

00:25:07.289 --> 00:25:08.289
Right?
对吧？

00:25:08.289 --> 00:25:09.289
Right?
对吧？

00:25:09.289 --> 00:25:10.809
And it's like the storytelling is what makes it magic.
而且可以说，正是这种讲故事的方式让它变得如此神奇。

00:25:10.809 --> 00:25:14.329
The way we shape our story is what is magic.
我们塑造故事的方式才是神奇所在。

00:25:14.329 --> 00:25:29.769
The telling of the story itself is magic, especially when you're trying to tell your kids who you were and what your life was before they existed and what your heart was like and what you put out into the universe and what you got back.
讲述故事本身就是一种魔力，尤其是当你试图告诉你的孩子，在他们出生前你是谁，你的生活是怎样的，你的内心曾是怎样的，你向宇宙付出了什么，又得到了什么。

00:25:29.769 --> 00:25:40.890
It's so pure, Ted is so pure of heart in 2006 when this is happening and the act of telling his kids, the story is also in its own way very pure of heart and I believe that magic comes from that.
它是如此纯粹，2006年事情发生时，泰德的内心是如此纯粹，而他给孩子们讲故事的行为，某种程度上也极其纯粹，我相信这种魔力就来源于此。

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:41.890
Yeah.
是的。

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:48.730
And it's also like we become so dead into the miraculousness of the every day because we live in it every single day.
而且这就好像，我们对日常生活的奇迹感变得极其麻木，因为我们每天都身处其中。

00:25:48.730 --> 00:26:04.849
But if you were just dropped in here and you saw like trees and dolphins and ocean and constellations and like, it's a stuff, if you were like, that's how you make other humans, like it's so unbelievably miraculous.
但如果你被空投到这里，看到树木、海豚、海洋、星座，以及这一切，如果你心想，人类就是这样被创造出来的，这简直是不可思议的奇迹。

00:26:04.849 --> 00:26:07.049
Or what's that term, the pathetic fallacy?
或者那个叫什么来着，移情谬误？

00:26:07.049 --> 00:26:18.470
Do you know that literary term where it's raining outside and you think that it's reflective of your depressive state, but it's just, it's almost like a misassociation.
你知道那个文学术语吗？就是外面在下雨，你觉得那是你抑郁状态的映射，但其实它只是，这几乎就像是一种错误的联想。

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:20.049
But I believe in it.
但我相信这一点。

00:26:20.049 --> 00:26:24.849
I feel like, yeah, some days you feel blue and the weather reflects that and it's just for you.
我觉得，是的，有时你感到沮丧，而天气恰好反映了这一点，而且它就像是专门为你准备的一样。

00:26:24.849 --> 00:26:28.730
It's okay, I don't think that's narcissistic or whatever.
没关系，我不觉得这有什么自恋之类的。

00:26:28.730 --> 00:26:35.410
But do you know the famous David Foster Wallace graduation speech that he gave at Kenyon, which is commonly called This Is Water?
但你知道大卫·福斯特·华莱士在凯恩那所大学发表的著名毕业演讲吗？通常被称为《这是水》。

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:43.730
He tells that story of there's two men talking and they're talking about whether God is real.
他讲了这样一个故事：有两个人在一起聊天，讨论上帝是否真实存在。

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:57.049
And the non-believer says, I was lost in, I don't know, Alaska or the Arctic and I was stranded in a snowstorm and there were no humans around and I was sure I was gonna die and I cried out.
那个不信神的人说，我当时在，我不知道，阿拉斯加或者北极，我被困在暴风雪中，周围没有一个人，我确信自己死定了，于是我大声呼喊。

00:26:57.049 --> 00:27:02.049
I said, God, if you exist, save me.
我说：“上帝，如果你存在，救救我吧。”

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:08.470
And then the believer looks at him and says, well, you're here, so clearly you were saved.
然后那个信神的人看着他说：“嗯，你现在还活着，所以显然你得救了。”

00:27:08.470 --> 00:27:09.630
And he goes, no, it wasn't God.
而他说：“不，那不是上帝。”

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:12.710
There was just some Eskimos started to wander by and they saved me.
只是刚好有几个爱斯基摩人路过，他们救了我。

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:14.789
So it wasn't God, right?
所以那不是上帝，对吧？

00:27:14.789 --> 00:27:22.309
So it's like whatever angle you're looking at it from to confirm your bias or your previously held point of view you'll hook into.
所以这就好像无论你从哪个角度去看，来证实你的偏见或你先前持有的观点，你都会将其套用进去。

00:27:22.309 --> 00:27:38.109
But I've always loved that, I always, I do think that there is a theological, And I don't know if that's the right word, but there's a cosmic magical realism thing running through the pulse of how I met your mother that I think people really love.
